The function first checks if the input is zero and returns `"0 B"` directly. For non‑zero values it iteratively divides the number by 1024 while tracking the appropriate unit (B, KiB, MiB, GiB, TiB). The loop stops when the number is less than 1024 or when the unit index exceeds the list length. The final number is formatted with one decimal place unless the unit is plain bytes, in which case it is shown as an integer. This handles all edge cases such as very small numbers (showing as bytes) and very large numbers (capping at TiB).

