I will first determine the effective rotation amount by taking the modulo of k with the array length, which handles cases where k is larger than the array size or negative. Then I'll create a new array by slicing the tail of the array (the part that should move to the front) and concatenating it with the head (the part that stays in place). This approach ensures we don't modify the original array and works for all integer values of k.

ANSWER:
```javascript
function rotateArray(arr, k) {
  const n = arr.length;
  if (n === 0) return [];
  const effectiveK = ((k % n) + n) % n;
  if (effectiveK === 0) return [...arr];
  return [...arr.slice(-effectiveK), ...arr.slice(0, -effectiveK)];
}

module.exports = rotateArray;
```

