I'll create a FlatList that uses the item's id when available, but falls back to the index when the id is missing or null. This ensures each row has a unique key, preventing React warnings. I'll also handle the case where the id might be undefined by checking for its existence before using it.

ANSWER:
```jsx
import React from 'react';
import { FlatList, Text, View } from 'react-native';

const MyList = ({ data }) => {
  const keyExtractor = (item, index) => {
    return item.id?.toString() || index.toString();
  };

  const renderItem = ({ item }) => (
    <View>
      <Text>{item.name}</Text>
    </View>
  );

  return (
    <FlatList
      data={data}
      keyExtractor={keyExtractor}
      renderItem={renderItem}
    />
  );
};

export default MyList;
```

