I'll create a React Native component that uses useRef to store a mutable value that persists across renders without triggering re-renders. The useRef hook provides a stable reference to a value that doesn't cause re-renders when updated, making it ideal for storing values that need to persist between renders but shouldn't trigger UI updates. One edge case to consider is that the value stored in useRef won't be updated in the component's render output, so any UI that needs to display this value must use useEffect or other state management techniques to reflect changes.

ANSWER:
import React, { useRef, useEffect } from 'react';
import { View, Text, Button } from 'react-native';

const MutableValueComponent = () => {
  const countRef = useRef(0);
  const [displayCount, setDisplayCount] = useState(0);

  const incrementCount = () => {
    countRef.current += 1;
    setDisplayCount(countRef.current);
  };

  return (
    <View>
      <Text>Count: {displayCount}</Text>
      <Button title="Increment" onPress={incrementCount} />
    </View>
  );
};

export default MutableValueComponent;
